  "description": "O3' selects a block, but NOT the bound state's block. The objective t_lambda = w_lambda * chi^lambda(W_atom) factors into an atom-INDEPENDENT weight and an atom-dependent character; measured over all six committed rows, argmin(t) == argmin(w) every time, so the character never moves the selection. Two atoms at n=3 and two at n=9 give the SAME block with DIFFERENT prices. Mechanism UNEXPLAINED: the first spread-dominance account is refuted here -- chi-spread exceeds w-spread at n=9 and the argmin is unmoved anyway. THE MECHANISM THAT DOES hold is an ANTI-CORRELATION between w and chi: concordant partition pairs are 9/49 = 18% against 24.5 expected, so a large character lands preferentially on a small weight and the product is FLATTENED relative to w on every row (never amplified) -- chi is a partial cancellation of the ordering it would have to reverse, not an independent perturbation. Exact distribution-free tail, one row per distinct n and the least extreme within each: 343/518400 ~ 6.6e-4. Cancellation is INCOMPLETE (t-spread 1.35x-10.2x), so w's argmin survives. WHY the anti-correlation holds is OPEN. CONSEQUENCE: PR #6752's reading of the selection as 'which Wedderburn blocks the bound state saturates' is WRONG and is corrected; the pricing result (y_0* nonzero and atom-dependent, where the full LP has y_0* = 0 identically) STANDS.",
